From 6d0bcb1a743b5bb4783a12a5bf7a7a251acd4caf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jayneelatintel <jayneel.vora@intel.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 06:40:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 15/21] dist-tp: prompt-size mesh on ND, both links at once
 (LLAMA_DIST_TP_MESH_ND_LARGE)

With LLAMA_DIST_TP_MESH=1 the 3-rank mesh only carried payloads up to
64 KiB (decode); every prompt batch kept the store-and-forward ring,
which sends the payload twice over one link in two dependent steps
while the reverse direction of every cable sits idle.

LLAMA_DIST_TP_MESH_ND_LARGE=1 sends large payloads through the mesh
too, with every direction on NetworkDirect. dist_nd_exchange_pair()
sends one message to both peers and receives one from each in a single
non-blocking loop: 256 KiB chunks are interleaved across the two
connections, received chunks go straight into the caller's buffers (no
spill copy), and acks leave as soon as a window's worth is consumed. It
never blocks on one connection while the other could move, apart from
the existing wait for the previous ack's own send completion. Each link
then carries the payload once in each direction at the same time.

Only taken when RDMA is on, LLAMA_DIST_TP_RDMA_MULTICHUNK=1,
LLAMA_DIST_TP_RDMA_MAX_BYTES=0, and no relay, reverse-TCP or TCP large
mesh is set; all read from the environment, so every rank decides the
same way. Otherwise the banner says why and large payloads keep the
ring. A rank whose ND connection is down fails the collective: there is
no TCP path for these payloads.

The sum order is unchanged ((own + prev) + next, served through the
existing two-step mesh handoff), so the output stays bit-identical to
the ring. nd_flush is split into the window wait and nd_post_staged();
every existing path behaves as before. Unset, nothing changes.
